  "body": "Update the machine program gate (`crates/app/src/machine/judge.rs` and `mod.rs`) to verify XYZAC paths on final post-IK machine coordinates instead of raw part-frame positions or canonical `[A, B, 0]` angles.\n\n### Context\n- `pose_problems` in `crates/app/src/machine/judge.rs` checks canonical `[A, B, C]` (`C = 0`, `crates/cl/src/cl/pose.rs`) directly without IK, so it wrongly rejects valid AC poses with non-zero canonical `B`.\n- `check_ops` in `crates/app/src/machine/mod.rs` checks `X Y Z` travels on raw world coordinates minus `origin` and checks `feed_ceiling` on linear deltas only, missing pivot-swing X/Y/Z excursion and rotary angular speeds.\n- Depends on schema (#1962), AC IK solver (#1965), and `G93` timing (#1966).\n\n### Requirements\n- Call the shared Rust AC IK solver in `machine::judge` and `machine::check_ops` for non-RTCP multi-axis tables (matching `lathe_stroke.rs` single-source-of-truth pattern).\n- Judge `X, Y, Z, A, C` travels on the exact compensated machine segment sequence produced by the solver, and refuse unreachable tool orientations by name.\n- Check both linear and rotary axis speed ceilings (`max_normal_speed`, `max_rapid_speed`) along each segment.\n- Keep existing 3-axis and `tilthead.mch` controller-RTCP checks unchanged.\n\nDone means: Legal XYZAC 5-axis programs pass `machine::check_ops`, out-of-stroke pivot swings and rotary over-speeds trigger named refusals matching posted coordinates, and 3-axis and `tilthead.mch` tests stay green.\n\nPart of #1958\n",
